        "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n-4)!$ for any $n\\ge 5?$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-4)!=(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-4)! & =(n-4)\\cdot \\overset{(n-5)!}{\\overset{\u23de}{(n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$",
